  tudor  um going back centuries earlier  the civil war in england between stephen  and matilda which was over  whether in fact a woman could in fact  inherit the english crown  and they passed her over but she was  pissed about it and went to war and  you know that war ripped england apart  for a generation  um so there have been many strong women  in history even though  um the society was by no means an  egalitarian one  and i try to reflect both things in my  books to  to show a society that doesn't give  women equal rights and  the frustrations and the pain and the  difficulties that cause many of the  women but  also to show certain women of strong  character or ability  um dealing with that in one way or  another and what kind of accommodations  they  they make with that and how they succeed  despite these restrictions and  the cost because there's always a kind  of a cost when you  buck your social expectations um  but it's certainly one of the things  that uh  that i deal with in uh in the books one  of the things that pleases me both about  these books is that i do have  so many uh women readers and that so  many of them come up to me and tell me  they  they do like the uh the women characters  in the books  um because that is something i work on  and um  it's not true of a lot of fantasy i  think a lot of fantasy  either  neglects women or if they do use women  like the women warrior has become sort  of a stock almost cartoonish character  the  the babe in the brass bikini uh which is  sort of impractical um so i'm  trying to uh trying to deal with these  things and turn these on their head and  offer  a wide variety of different women who  are different from each other  and reflect different ways of dealing  with a kind of society like that
 

